, Play B2, Play B4\n\nSo these six tiles are in hand and can be discarded.\n\nThat means your hand contains at least: B1, W5, W3, B7, B2, B4 \u2014 and possibly one more? Or is it 6?\n\nWait \u2014 in Chinese Mahjong, after drawing, you have 14 tiles before discarding.\n\nBut before drawing, you have 13.\n\nAssuming it's **before drawing**, you have 13 tiles: 6 in hand (from valid plays), plus 2 melds \u00d7 3 = 6 tiles \u2192 12, missing one.\n\nAh \u2014 **[B9,B9,B9]** is one meld (3 tiles), **[W3,W4,W5]** is second (3 tiles) \u2192 total 6 melded tiles.\n\nThen hand must be 7 tiles.\n\nBut valid actions list 6 possible discards \u2192 implies hand is 6 tiles? Contradiction.\n\nAlternative interpretation: The \"hand tiles\" list shows **all tiles currently held**, including duplicates, and the melds are separate.\n\nList: [W3:1; W5:1; B1:1; B2:1; B4:1; B7:3] \u2192 sum to 8 tiles.\n\nWith two melds (6 tiles), total 14 tiles \u2192 so you\u2019ve already drawn, and are about to discard.\n\nYes \u2014 makes sense: you drew a tile, now have 14, must discard one.\n\nSo hand: 8 tiles? No \u2014 14 total tiles: 6 in melds, 8 in hand \u2192 too many.\n\nUnless one of the melds is a kong? No \u2014 both are pungs.\n\nWait \u2014 **Peng B9** in history: you Peng\u2019d B9 \u2014 meaning you declared a pung of B9 using discard.\n\nBut then you had to discard a tile \u2014 \"Play T4\" after.\n\nBut the meld [B9,B9,B9] is 3 tiles.\n\nSimilarly, [W3,W4,W5] is 3 tiles.\n\nTotal melded: 6 tiles.\n\nHand: 8 tiles \u2192 14 total \u2192 **only possible if you just drew a tile, making 14, and must discard one**.\n\nSo yes \u2014 you are at decision point after drawing.\n\nNow, which tile did you draw? Not specified.\n\nBut your hand has: W3, W5, B1, B2, B4, B7\u00d73\n\nAnd valid discards: Play B1; Play W5; Play W3; Play B7; Play B2; Play B4\n\nSo all six types are discardable \u2192 you hold at least one of each.\n\nCount: B7\u00d73, others \u00d71 \u2192 total 3 + 5 = 8 tiles in hand.\n\nMelds: 6 tiles \u2192 14 total \u2192 yes.\n\nSo you drew a tile recently to make 14.\n\nPossibly you drew one of: B7 (to make triple), or B1/B2/B4/W3/W5.\n\nBut W3 and W5 were used in Chi \u2014 how can they be back?\n\nAh \u2014 unless you drew another W3 or W5 later.\n\nPossible \u2014 tiles can be drawn again.\n\nBut you used W3 and W5 to form [W3,W4,W5] via Chi, so those specific tiles are in the meld.\n\nIf you later drew another W3 or W5, they would be in hand.\n\nIs that allowed?\n\nYes \u2014 there are 4 copies of each tile.\n\nSo you could have used one W3 in the chow, and still hold another W3.\n\nBut the meld [W3,W4,W5] requires one W3, one W4, one W5.\n\nYou claimed W4 via Chi, so you must have contributed W3 and W5 from hand at that time.\n\nAfter that, if you draw another W3 or W5 later, you can have them in hand.\n\nSo current hand: you have **a new W3 and/or W5** that you drew after the Chi.\n\nSimilarly, B7\u00d73 suggests you collected them over time.\n\nSo current hand: W3, W5, B1, B2, B4, B7, B7, B7 \u2192 8 tiles.\n\nYou must discard one.\n\nNow evaluate:\n\n- **B7\u00d73**: Strong concealed pung. Can contribute to:\n  - **All Pungs** (6 pts)\n  - **Two Concealed Pungs** (2 pts) \u2014 need another concealed pung\n  - **Outside Hand**? B7 is not terminal (7 is terminal? Yes \u2014 1 and 9 are terminals. 7 is simple.)\n    - So B7 is **simple**, not terminal/honor \u2192 not helpful for Outside Hand.\n  - So better for **All Simples** (2 pts) or **All Chows** (2 pts), but you already have a pung \u2192 not All Chows.\n  - So if you keep B7 pung, you're going for **All Pungs** or similar.\n\n- Other tiles: B1 (terminal), B2, B4, W3 (middle), W5 (middle), B1 \u2014 all scattered.\n\nYou already have:\n- Exposed pung: B9 (terminal)\n- Exposed chow: W3-W4-W5 (middle flush)\n- Concealed pung: B7\u00d73\n\nSo your hand structure is mixed.\n\nPotential patterns:\n- **All Pungs**: you have two pungs (B9, B7), need two more. But you have a chow \u2014 unless you convert or abandon.\n  - But chow is already exposed \u2014 can't change.\n  - So **cannot have All Pungs** because of the chow.\n- **Half Flush**: possible \u2014 mostly W and B, some honors? You have no honors.\n  - But W and B are two different suits \u2014 not half flush unless one suit dominates.\n  - Half Flush: one suit + honors.\n  - Here, you have **Characters (W)** and **Dots (B)** \u2014 two suits \u2192 not Half Flush.\n  - Unless one suit is primary.\n  - Let's count:\n    - W: in meld: W3,W4,W5 (3), in hand: W3, W5 \u2192 total W: 5 tiles\n    - B: in meld: B9\u00d73 (3), in hand: B1,B2,B4,B7\u00d73 \u2192 6 \u2192 total B: 9\n    - So **B (Dots) is dominant** \u2192 possibly aiming for **Full Flush in Dots**?\n      - But you have W tiles in meld and hand \u2192 unless you discard W3/W5.\n      - If you discard W3 and W5, you eliminate W tiles.\n      - Then hand becomes: B1, B2, B4, B7\u00d73 \u2192 and melds: [B9,B9,B9], [W3,W4,W5]\n      - But [W3,W4,W5] is still there \u2014 it's a meld in Characters \u2014 so you still have two suits.\n      - Therefore, **cannot achieve Full Flush or Half Flush**.\n\nAnother idea: **Upper Tiles** (24 pts): only 7,8,9.\n\nYou have:\n- B7\u00d73 (good)\n- B9\u00d73 (good)\n- Others: B1, B2, B4, W3, W5 \u2192 all low/middle \u2192 not Upper Tiles.\n\n**Lower Tiles**: 1,2,3 \u2192 B1, B2, but B4, B7, B9 break it.\n\n**Middle Tiles**: 4,5,6 \u2192 only B4, W5 \u2192 not enough.\n\n**Pure Straight**: needs 123, 456, 789 in same suit.\n\nIn B (Dots):\n- You have B1, B2 \u2192 can form 123 if get B3\n- B4 \u2192 can form 456 if get B5,B6\n- B7, B9 \u2192 B7+B9 \u2192 missing B8 for 789\n\nClose, but missing key tiles.\n\nMoreover, you have a pung of B9 \u2014 so B9 is fixed.\n\nCould go for **Three-Suited Terminal Chows**? Needs 123 and 789 in two suits, pair of 5 in third.\n\nNot applicable.\n\nBetter: **Mixed Triple Chow**? Three chows of same numbers in different suits.\n\nUnlikely.\n\nBack to discards.\n\nBest move is to **keep B7\u00d73** \u2014 it's a concealed pung, scores at least **2 pts for Dragon Pung? No \u2014 Dragon is J, not B**.\n\nB7 is **not dragon**.\n\nSo **Pung of Terminals or Honors**? B7 is number 7 \u2014 **not terminal** (terminals are 1 and 9). So no.\n\nSo **B7 pung is just a normal pung of simples**.\n\nIt contributes to:\n- **All Pungs** (6 pts) \u2014 but you have a chow \u2192 invalid.\n- Otherwise, no direct pattern.\n\nBut wait \u2014 **Concealed Pung** counts for **Two Concealed Pungs** (2 pts) if you have two.\n\nCurrently, you have:\n- B7\u00d73: concealed\n- B9\u00d73: exposed (from Peng) \u2192 not concealed\n- W3-W4-W5: exposed chow\n\nSo only **one concealed pung** (B7)\n\nTo get **Two Concealed Pungs**, you need another concealed pung.\n\nBut your other tiles are singles.\n\nAlternatively, aim for **Seven Pairs**? Unlikely \u2014 you have triples.\n\nOr **Chicken Hand** with minimal fan.\n\nBut let\u2019s check **valid patterns** you might complete.\n\nPerhaps **All Simples** (2 pts): no terminals or honors.\n\nTerminals: B1, B9, W3? W3 is 3 \u2014 simple? No \u2014 **simples are 2-8**, terminals are 1 and 9.\n\nSo B1 and B9 are terminals.\n\nYou have B1 in hand, B9 in pung \u2192 so **have terminals** \u2192 cannot have All Simples.\n\nSimilarly, **No Honors** is true, but only 1 pt.\n\nAnother idea: **One Voided Suit** (1 pt) \u2014 you have W and B \u2192 missing T (Bamboos)? Or do you?\n\nOther players have T melds:\n- Next: [W5,W6,W7], [T3,T4,T5]\n- Across: [T6,T7,T8], [W6,W7,W8]\n- Previous: [W9,W9,W9]\n\nSo T is present on table.\n\nBut \u201cOne Voided Suit\u201d means your hand uses only two suits.\n\nYou have W and B \u2014 so yes, **missing T** \u2192 qualifies for **One Voided Suit** (1 pt)\n\nBut not much.\n\nNow, look at discards:\n\n- **Play B1**: B1 is terminal, hard to use, no chow potential (need B2,B3 for 123, you have B2 but no B3). B3 is available (unshown: B3:2), but risky. Discarding B1 removes a terminal.\n- **Play B2**: B2 can be part of 123 or 234. You have B1 and B4, so B2 connects B1 and B4 slightly, but without B3, weak.\n- **Play B4**: B4 can be in 234, 345, 456. You have B2, B7 \u2014 no direct link.\n- **Play W3 or W5**: both are in the middle range. W3 could be in 123, 234, 345; W5 in 345, 456, 567. But you have no other W except these. And W3/W5 are not in short supply (unshown: W3:2, W5:2), so safe to discard.\n- **Play B7**: dangerous \u2014 B7 is in short supply (unshown: B7:4, but you have 3, so only one left). If you discard B7, you give up a concealed pung and risk someone else using it. But if you discard one B7, you still have two \u2014 can form a pair later.\n\nBut you must discard one tile.\n\nBest strategy: **minimize damage to potential patterns**.\n\nSince you have a concealed pung of B7, and B7 is not terminal, keeping it is good for flexibility.\n\nB1 is a terminal, and you already have a pung of B9 (terminal), so having B1 doesn't help much.\n\nDiscarding B1 reduces terminal count, but you still have B9.\n\nBut **no pattern requires avoiding terminals**.\n\nHowever, **Edge Wait** or **Closed Wait** might apply later, but not now.\n\nThe best discard is **B1**, because:\n- It\u2019s a terminal\n- Least connected to other tiles\n- Doesn't contribute to any strong pattern\n- Keeps B7 pung intact\n- Keeps B2 and B4 for potential 234 or 123/456 draws\n\nCompare to discarding W3 or W5: they are part of Characters, which you have in a chow, but no other support.\n\nBut W tiles are not helping unify the hand.\n\nSince B is dominant (9 tiles), better to focus on Dots.\n\nBut you can't remove the W chow.\n\nSo accept mixed hand.\n\nFinal choice: **Play B1**\n\nIt\u2019s the safest throw, least useful tile.\n\nOther options:\n- **Play B2 or B4**: might break potential chow, but no immediate benefit.\n- **Play B7**: wasteful \u2014 lose a strong meld core.\n\n**Answer: Play B1**\n\nEvaluation: Among the valid discards, B1 is a terminal tile with poor connectivity (lacking B3 for 123 or 234 chows) and does not contribute to high-value patterns. Keeping B7\u00d73 preserves a concealed pung for potential scoring (e.g., Two Concealed Pungs if another concealed set forms). W3 and W5 are mid-game tiles but tied to an already-exposed chow. B2 and B4 offer slight sequence potential but are less critical than preserving the B7 pung. Discarding B1 minimizes strategic loss.\nReason: B1 is the least valuable tile in hand, being a terminal with no immediate use in forming high-scoring patterns, while retaining B7 maintains a concealed pung for future scoring opportunities.\nAnswer: Play B1"}
